@book{deleuze1987plateaus,
  author = {Deleuze, Gilles and Guattari, F{\'e}lix},
  title = {A Thousand Plateaus: Capitalism and Schizophrenia},
  publisher = {University of Minnesota Press},
  year = {1987},
  address = {Minneapolis},
  note = {Translated by Brian Massumi; originally published in French as Mille Plateaux (Paris: Les {\'E}ditions de Minuit, 1980). Plateau 9, “1933: Micropolitics and Segmentarity.” Molar and molecular are two kinds of segmentarity, rigid and supple, both running at every scale -- not two sizes; an earlier version of this note said state-level and micro-level and was wrong. The same plateau supplies the micro-fascisms that resonate, and the contrast between the conservative totalitarian state and fascism's line of abolition. Also cited for Plateau 13, “The Apparatus of Capture,” whose propositions on the capitalist axiomatic supply the corrected test for the first structural feature: an axiomatic does not refuse a demand, it adds an axiom, modifying its own rules so the demand can be met while nothing that produced the demand is settled. Also cited for Plateau 7, “Year Zero: Faciality,” pages 167--191, where the face is not a natural given but is produced by an abstract machine of faciality (visag{\'e}it{\'e}) that overcodes the head into a surface of signification. The quoted sentence on racism as the determination of degrees of deviance is at page 178, wording agent-checked against two independent scholarly sources quoting it identically. The related claims that the face is not universal but is White Man himself, and that the face is a politics, are used in paraphrase and cited to the plateau rather than to a page: secondary sources place them variously and the pages were not agent-verified. Plateau 9 carries two further uses. The four dangers of the line of flight -- Fear, which clings to the molar organization and its binary machines; Clarity, where the line acquires its own micro-segmentations and micro-Oedipuses crop up; Power, where the party able to move on both lines wants to hold both still; and the great Disgust, abolition pure and simple, the passion of abolition, which turns the line of flight into a line of death -- are the account section 3.5 uses, and the fourth is the same line already cited from this plateau as the line of abolition, which is why section 3.5 treats it as a recognition rather than a new claim. Plateau 13 likewise carries a second use: there is enslavement when human beings are themselves constituent pieces of a machine they compose among themselves and with other things, under the control and direction of a higher unity, and subjection when the higher unity constitutes the human being as a subject linked to a new exterior object, which can be an animal, a tool, or even a machine. Section 6.4.1 uses the pair and the fact that both poles run together. Wording for the dangers and for the enslavement and subjection definitions was agent-checked against independent sources quoting the passages; both are cited at plateau level with no page asserted, which is this entry's convention.}
}

