  addendum = {Read from the report's own HTML full text at https://files.gao.gov/reports/GAO-26-107859/index.html; the PDF was never needed, and the product page alone is what defeated an earlier attempt. Scope: thirteen AI acquisitions at four agencies --- the Departments of Defense, Homeland Security and Veterans Affairs and the General Services Administration --- and, from that sample, “44 contracts and agreements awarded between September 2018 and February 2025.” **The forty-four are contracts and agreements, not acquisitions.** Cited for the other-transaction finding: “OTAs do not always include default provisions ordinarily in FAR contracts,” contracting officials deciding what protections to include, and GAO having “previously found that agencies' use of OTAs carries the risk of reduced accountability and transparency compared to FAR-based contracts.” Also for the vehicle behind the agreements section 10.3 turns on: “In July 2025, DOD awarded OTAs with \$200 million ceilings to four leading AI companies.” The six key procurement challenge areas identified by the selected agencies are access to subject matter experts; protections for government data and intellectual property rights; traditional acquisition time frames and contract approaches; requirements definition and contract terms; early testing and continuous evaluation; and artificial intelligence pricing and overall cost. The report's central finding, which the book does not use, is that the agencies were not yet systematically collecting lessons learned because their policies did not require it. Maven appears in it as the National Geospatial-Intelligence Agency's programme, with lessons-learned sections citing its practices; that material is not used either. Agent-verified 2026-09-18.}
