  year = {1985},
  address = {Princeton},
  note = {Edited and translated by Edwin Curley; contains the Ethics, composed in Latin and published posthumously in 1677. Cited by part and by definition, proposition or scholium rather than by page, the convention for this text, so the locators hold across other translations. Passages carrying the argument: III Def. 3, where an affect is an affection by which the body's power of acting is increased or diminished together with the idea of that affection; III P2 Schol., where nobody has yet determined what the body can do; III P6 and III P7, the conatus doctrine, where each thing as far as it can by its own power strives to persevere in its being and that striving is nothing but the thing's actual essence; III P4, no thing can be destroyed except through an external cause, which the derivation of P6 rests on; III P9 Schol., desire as appetite together with consciousness of the appetite; III P11 Schol., joy and sadness as the passage to a greater and a lesser perfection; and the physical digression after II P13, Lemmas 4--7, where an individual persists for as long as its parts preserve the same ratio of motion and rest. IV P31, where a thing is good insofar as it agrees with our nature, and IV P35--P37, where men agree in nature only insofar as they live by the guidance of reason and where the good a person of virtue wants for himself he wants for other men, were added at D-361; P35--P37 were agent-checked in the Latin and P37 against a quotation of Curley's English, Curley's volume not being to hand. Quotations follow Curley's wording, agent-checked against it.}
