  note    = {Cited for a sender who chooses what evidence gets generated raising the rate at which a rational receiver takes the sender’s preferred action, although the receiver’s expected posterior equals its prior, and for the commitment that result assumes. Abstract: “When is it possible for one person to persuade another to change her action? We consider a symmetric information model where a sender chooses a signal to reveal to a receiver, who then takes a noncontractible action that affects the welfare of both players. We derive necessary and sufficient conditions for the existence of a signal that strictly benefits the sender. We characterize sender-optimal signals.” The leading example (page 2591): prosecutor and judge “share a prior belief Pr(guilty) = 0.3”; the judge convicts when her posterior is at least 0.5; the prosecutor’s optimal investigation “leads the judge to convict with probability 60 percent. Note that the judge knows 70 percent of defendants are innocent, yet she convicts 60 percent of them! She does so even though she is fully aware that the investigation was designed to maximize the probability of conviction.” In that example the concave closure “V is equal to 2μ when μ ≤ 0.5” (page 2597), that is, the prior divided by the threshold. The sender’s problem is a search over distributions of posteriors “subject to the constraint that the expected posterior is equal to the prior” (page 2592). On commitment: “A key assumption of our model is that Sender cannot distort or conceal information once the signal realization is known” (page 2591), and “Receiver observes Sender’s choice of the signal and a signal realization” (page 2594); the authors call the assumption “strong” (page 2598) and name legally mandated disclosure, as in \emph{Brady v. Maryland}, as a setting where it holds (page 2599), in section I.C, “The Commitment Assumption.” Bibliographic details agent-verified 2026-09-25 against Crossref and OpenAlex; quotations agent-verified 2026-09-26 against the published article as a PDF hosted on the second author’s Stanford site (web.stanford.edu/\textasciitilde gentzkow/research/BayesianPersuasion.pdf), which carries the AER pagination. The general bound for two states and two actions, the prior divided by the threshold, is not stated in that form in the passages read; it follows from the example’s concave closure and from the expected-posterior constraint. The online Appendix and the applications in section V were not read.}
}

