  note        = {General List No.~95, on the request of the United Nations General Assembly (resolution 49/75~K of 15 December 1994). Cited for the relation between human-rights law and the law of armed conflict in hostilities. Paragraph~25: “The Court observes that the protection of the International Covenant of Civil and Political Rights does not cease in times of war, except by operation of Article~4 of the Covenant whereby certain provisions may be derogated from in a time of national emergency. Respect for the right to life is not, however, such a provision. In principle, the right not arbitrarily to be deprived of one’s life applies also in hostilities. The test of what is an arbitrary deprivation of life, however, then falls to be determined by the applicable lex specialis, namely, the law applicable in armed conflict which is designed to regulate the conduct of hostilities. Thus whether a particular loss of life, through the use of a certain weapon in warfare, is to be considered an arbitrary deprivation of life contrary to Article~6 of the Covenant, can only be decided by reference to the law applicable in armed conflict and not deduced from the terms of the Covenant itself.” Paragraph~25 speaks to the right to life only; the Court’s general statement of the three possible relations between the two bodies of law is in the later Wall opinion (I.C.J. Reports 2004, p.~136, para.~106), which was not read here. Agent-verified 2026-09-26 against two transcriptions of the opinion: the National Security Archive’s OCR text of the printed I.C.J. Reports issue, whose title page gives the official citation “I.C.J. Reports 1996, p.~226,” and the transcription hosted by NYU’s Institute for International Law and Justice; the two agree word for word on paragraph~25. Date, case number and requesting resolution agent-verified against the Court’s case page. The Court’s own PDF of the opinion refused automated retrieval (HTTP 403) and was not read, so the page of the Reports o