  note    = {Cited for the conflict of interest in the issuer-pays model of credit rating. White dates the change from “investor pays” to “issuer pays” to the early 1970s and says (p.~215) that it “opened the door to potential conflicts of interest: A rating agency might shade its rating upward so as to keep the issuer happy and forestall the issuer’s taking its rating business to a different rating agency.” He does not treat the erosion as automatic: “the rating agencies’ concerns about their long-run reputations apparently kept the actual conflicts in check for the first three decades,” helped by “thousands of corporate and government bond issuers” and by the transparency of “plain vanilla” debt (p.~215). The conflict broke through in mortgage-related securities, where a few high-volume securitizers had “a more powerful threat—to move all of its securitization business to a different rating agency” and the agencies were “under considerable financial pressure to give the answers that issuers wanted to hear” (p.~221). The record he cites: of CDO tranches issued 2005--2007 and rated AAA by Standard \& Poor’s, “90 percent … had been downgraded” by 30 June 2009 (p.~221, from the IMF), and in footnote~11 econometric studies supporting “the likelihood of ratings shopping.” Bibliographic details agent-verified 2026-09-26 against the AEA article page and Crossref; quotations against the published PDF as archived by the Wayback Machine (snapshot of 14 November 2025), the AEA’s own PDF link refusing automated retrieval. The studies White cites were not read.}
