  url          = {https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/23-939},
  urldate      = {2026-09-26},
  note         = {Cited for the scope of presidential immunity from criminal prosecution. Decided July 1, 2024; 6--3. Roberts, C.J., for the Court, joined in full by Thomas, Alito, Gorsuch and Kavanaugh, JJ., and by Barrett, J., except as to Part III--C; Thomas, J., concurring; Barrett, J., concurring in part; Sotomayor, J., dissenting, joined by Kagan and Jackson, JJ.; Jackson, J., dissenting. Absolute immunity (Part II--A, slip op.\ 6--9): “the President is absolutely immune from criminal prosecution for conduct within his exclusive sphere of constitutional authority,” authority that is “conclusive and preclusive”; the summary in Part IV speaks of “his core constitutional powers.” The powers the majority names as conclusive and preclusive are the pardon power, the power to remove executive officers, the power “to control recognition determinations” of foreign countries, and (Part III--B--1) the investigative and prosecutorial functions of the Justice Department. Commanding the Armed Forces appears only in an earlier list of the President’s duties, “They include, for instance, commanding the Armed Forces of the United States; granting reprieves and pardons \ldots,” and the majority does not place it among the conclusive and preclusive powers. Presumptive immunity (Part II--B, slip op.\ 9--15): “at least a presumptive immunity from criminal prosecution for a President’s acts within the outer perimeter of his official responsibility”; no immunity for unofficial acts (Part II--C). Evidence (Part III--C, slip op.\ 30--32, joined by five Justices): “Use of evidence about such conduct, even when an indictment alleges only unofficial conduct, would thereby heighten the prospect that the President’s official decisionmaking will be distorted”; n.\,3 lets the prosecutor “point to the public record to show the fact that the President performed the official act” but not “admit testimony or private records of the President or his advisers probing the official act itself.” Barrett, J., does not join Part III--C and on that point agrees with the dissent. The U.S. Reports citation is taken from \emph{Trump v.\ Cook} (2026), which cites “Trump v.\ United States, 603 U.\,S. 593.” Agent-verified 2026-09-26 against the Cornell LII text of the syllabus, the opinion of the Court and the Barrett concurrence. Not read: the Thomas concurrence, the dissents beyond the passages located by search, and the bound U.S. Reports; the slip-opinion pin pages are the syllabus’s and the Barrett concurrence’s page references, not read off the slip PDF.},
}

@misc{usc1304judgmentfund,
  author = {{United States Congress}},
  title = {Judgments, Awards, and Compromise Settlements (the Judgment Fund)},
  year = {1982},
  howpublished = {31 U.S.C. \S\,1304; Pub. L. No.\ 97-258, \S\,1, 96 Stat. 917 (1982), as amended; derived from Act of July 27, 1956, ch.\ 748, \S\,1302, 70 Stat. 694},
  url = {https://uscode.house.gov/view.xhtml?req=(title:31+section:1304+edition:prelim)},
  urldate = {2026-09-26},
  note = {Cited for the design of the Judgment Fund as a standing appropriation for judgments against the United States. Subsection (a): “Necessary amounts are appropriated to pay final judgments, awards, compromise settlements, and interest and costs specified in the judgments or otherwise authorized by law when— (1) payment is not otherwise provided for; (2) payment is certified by the Secretary of the Treasury; and (3) the judgment, award, or settlement is payable— (A) under section 2414, 2517, 2672, or 2677 of title 28” (with further heads in (B)--(D)). Treasury’s regulation gives the phrase the manuscript uses: “The Judgment Fund is a permanent, indefinite appropriation which is available to pay many judicially and administratively ordered monetary awards against the United States” (31 C.F.R. \S\,256.1(a)). The fund pays only awards payable under a statute listed in (a)(3) and not payable from another appropriation, so a new cause of action reaches it only if the statute creating it routes its awards there. Certification of payment passed from the Comptroller General to the Secretary of the Treasury by Pub. L. No.\ 104-316, \S\,202(m), 110 Stat. 3843 (1996). Under current practice the losing side asks for payment: “The Department of Justice must normally submit the request for payment from the Judgment Fund” (31 C.F.R. \S\,256.10(a)); for Court of Federal Claims judgments the statute itself provides for payment “on presentation to the Secretary of the Treasury of a certification of the judgment by the clerk and chief judge of the court” (28 U.S.C. \S\,2517(a)). Agent-verified 2026-09-26 against the text and amendment notes of 31 U.S.C. \S\,1304 at uscode.house.gov, 28 U.S.C. \S\S\,2414 and 2517 and 31 C.F.R. \S\S\,256.1, 256.10 and 256.11 at Cornell LII, and Treasury Financial Manual vol.\ I, pt.\ 6, ch.\ 3100, \S\,3130.10. Not read: the 1956 and 1982 enactments as printed in the Statutes at Large, and the GAO Principles of Federal Appropriations Law chapter on the Judgment Fund.}
}

