  note         = {Cited for the major-questions doctrine. Decided June 30, 2022; 6--3. Roberts, C.J., for the Court, joined by Thomas, Alito, Gorsuch, Kavanaugh and Barrett, JJ.; Gorsuch, J., concurring, joined by Alito, J.; Kagan, J., dissenting, joined by Breyer and Sotomayor, JJ. The opinion names the doctrine and answers the dissent’s objection to the label: it “took hold because it refers to an identifiable body of law that has developed over a series of significant cases all addressing a particular and recurring problem: agencies asserting highly consequential power beyond what Congress could reasonably be understood to have granted”; the agency “must point to ‘clear congressional authorization’ for the power it claims.” Holds that \S\,111(d) of the Clean Air Act did not authorize the Clean Power Plan’s generation-shifting emissions caps (slip op.\ 16--31). Agent-verified 2026-09-26 against the Cornell LII text of the syllabus and the searched passages of the opinion of the Court. Not read: the concurrence and the dissent. The U.S. Reports citation comes from \emph{FCC v.\ Consumers’ Research} (2025), which cites “West Virginia v.\ EPA, 597 U.\,S. 697,” not from the bound volume.},
